.TH LIVEFEED 2
.SH NAME
livefeed \- read a live video feed across a network
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "livefeed.m";
livefeed := load Livefeed Livefeed->PATH;
Edge: import livefeed;

init:    fn();
status:  fn(feed: string): (int, int);
pread:   fn(fd: ref Sys->FD, b: array of byte, n: int, off: big): int;
read:    fn(fd: ref Sys->FD, b: array of byte, off: big, ways: int): (int, string);

Edge: adt {
	feed:   string;
	fps:    int;
	lf:     int;
	lt:     int;
	new:    fn(feed: string, fps: int): ref Edge;
	newest: fn(e: self ref Edge): int;
	due:    fn(e: self ref Edge, n: int): int;
	again:  fn(e: self ref Edge): int;
	set:    fn(e: self ref Edge, lf, lt: int);
	next:   fn(e: self ref Edge, f, behind: int): int;
};
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
A feed served in the
.IR vid9p (4)
schema numbers its frames, and a reader at the live edge reads frame
.I n
as it is made.
Across a network, over
.IR sdg (2)
or TCP, a reader that asks the feed's
.B status
where the edge is after every frame spends a round trip on that alone, and
one that reads a frame's bytes one request after another spends several:
at 30 frames a second it falls behind.
This module is what
.IR wm-camera (1)
and
.IR vidjpeg (1)
read a feed with instead.
.PP
.B Status
returns the newest frame
.I feed
.B /status
names
.RB ( frames=
less one, or
.B first=
when nothing older is kept)
and
.B sys->millisec()
just before the read that told it was sent; the frame was made no later
than that read reached the feed.
It returns \-1 for the frame if status could not be read, or names no
frames.
.PP
An
.B Edge
keeps the live edge by the clock: from a frame known to exist
.RI ( lf )
at a time
.RI ( lt )
and the feed's rate
.RI ( fps ,
from its
.BR fmt ).
.B Edge.new
takes it from
.BR status ,
and is nil if that could not be read.
.B Newest
is the newest frame by the clock;
.B due
the milliseconds until frame
.I n
is due (0 or less once it is).
.B Again
takes the edge from
.B status
again: a reader calls it when a frame has expired (it is behind what the
feed keeps), not on other errors, since under loss a status read is itself
delayed, and an edge taken from every one runs ahead and skips frames that
could have been shown.
If status cannot be read,
.B again
returns 0 and leaves the edge as it was.
.B Set
does the same with a status read made elsewhere (in a process of the
caller's own).
.PP
.B Next
is the frame for a reader that has just read frame
.I f
to read next:
.IR f +1,
unless the reader is more than
.I behind
frames further back from the edge than it has been in the last second or
two, when it is the edge less that usual lag.
Across a network a frame comes round trips after it is made (five frames'
worth at 30 frames a second and a 100 ms round trip); a reader that
skipped whenever it was a few frames behind the clock would skip every
frame fetched for it.
.PP
.B Pread
is
.BR sys->pread ,
except that a read answered
.L "reply gone"
(sdg's: its reply was lost and is no longer in the server's cache) is sent
again, up to
.B GONETRIES
times in all.
A frame's bytes are read at known offsets, so a read sent again cannot do
anything twice.
Any other error is returned at once.
.PP
.B Read
reads
.BI len " b"
bytes at
.I off
as
.I ways
pieces at once, whatever their size, each a loop of
.BR pread .
Its result is
.IR parread (2)'s:
the bytes read contiguously from
.I off
and the first error in the order of the pieces.
.SH EXAMPLE
The reading loop of a viewer at the live edge, three frames' slack:
.EX
	edge := Edge.new(feed, fps);
	f := edge.newest();
	for(;;){
		(n, e) := livefeed->read(fd, buf, big f * big len buf, 4);
		if(e == "frame expired"){
			edge.again();
			f = edge.newest();
			continue;
		}
		if(n < len buf){
			# lost to the transport: this frame, not the feed
			f = edge.newest();
			continue;
		}
		show(f);
		f = edge.next(f, 3);
	}
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/livefeed.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR parread (2),
.IR sdg (2),
.IR vid9p (4),
.IR videnc (1),
.IR vidjpeg (1)
.SH BUGS
The clock is the reader's: across machines it drifts from the feed's by
the clocks' difference in rate, which
.B again
corrects when a frame expires.
